<details id="calcRef"><summary>Mission calculations (optional reference)</summary>
<div class="calcCard"><h3>Radiation exposure</h3><p><code>D<sub>total</sub> = Ḋ × t</code></p><p><b>D<sub>total</sub></b> is accumulated radiation dose, <b>Ḋ</b> is dose rate, and <b>t</b> is exposure duration. This multiplication assumes the dose rate stays constant; when it changes, the dose must be accumulated across the changing rates over time.</p></div>
<div class="calcCard"><h3>Food consumption</h3><p><code>M<sub>food</sub> = N × R<sub>f</sub> × T</code></p><p><b>M<sub>food</sub></b> is total food in kg; <b>N</b> is astronaut count; <b>R<sub>f</sub></b> is daily food per astronaut in kg/person/day; <b>T</b> is mission duration in days. The units multiply to kg. No numerical example is shown because a daily ration input is not specified here.</p></div>
<div class="calcCard"><h3>Equivalent System Mass (ESM)</h3><p><b>Image transcription (not verified):</b> <code>ESM = M + WV<sub>eq</sub> + PP<sub>eq</sub> + CC<sub>eq</sub> + CTDCT<sub>eq</sub></code>. The image’s symbols and definitions are ambiguous, so this expression should not be used as an authoritative equation.</p><p>NASA’s <i>Advanced Life Support Equivalent System Mass Guidelines</i> describes ESM conceptually as system mass plus mass-equivalent contributions for pressurized volume, power, cooling, and crew time. The conversion factors and complete accounting depend on the analysis and mission. <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20040021355/downloads/20040021355.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Technical Memorandum 2003-212278</a>.</p></div>
</details>
<details><summary>Where the science comes from</summary>
<p>The facts in “Did you know?” boxes come from these NASA sources. The game’s numbers are simplified so kids can play it, and lunar days are squeezed short. No live NASA feed is used: storm days are random but follow the real idea that the Sun is stormier at solar maximum. The 1972 storm fact is archived history, not live data.</p>
<ol>
<li>NASA, “Living and Working on the Moon”</li>
<li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moon/nasas-moon-orbiter-spots-new-once-in-century-moon-crater/">NASA Science, “NASA’s Moon Orbiter Spots New, ‘Once-in-Century’ Moon Crater”</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/missions/2020/10/28/lunar-living-nasas-artemis-base-camp-concept/">NASA, “Lunar Living: NASA’s Artemis Base Camp Concept”</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/reference/moonbase-environment/">NASA, “The Lunar South Pole Region”</a></li>
<li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/heliophysics/how-nasa-will-protect-astronauts-from-space-radiation-at-the-moon/">NASA Science, “How NASA Will Protect Astronauts From Space Radiation at the Moon”</a></li>
<li>K. Ryder and M. Campola, “The Lunar Radiation Environment,” NASA GSFC, 2022</li></ol>
<p>Communication delay is our own calculation (240,000 miles at light speed, about 1.3 seconds one way), not a NASA quote.</p></details>
</div>

